Looking at the failure, the root cause is Salesforce's queueable chain-depth limit (which is tightly constrained in Developer Edition/scratch orgs). During `Test.stopTest()`, chained `System.enqueueJob` calls made directly from within an executing `Queueable.execute()` accumulate stack depth across the whole chain and blow past the limit long before 50,000 records (250 batches) are processed.

The fix is to break the recursive chain into genuinely separate asynchronous transactions using a `Finalizer`. A finalizer always runs in its own transaction *after* the queueable's transaction completes (even under `Test.stopTest()`), which resets the stack-depth counter for each hop, allowing deep chains to complete safely. When `execute()` is invoked directly (not via the real async framework, e.g. the hidden `execute(null)` call), the platform-supplied `QueueableContext` will be `null`, so we detect that and fall back to a direct `enqueueJob` call instead of attaching a finalizer (which requires a real async job context).

